    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> on 10 April 1941 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> Military Tribunal later concluded it was not a sovereign state.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> regime killed an estimated 320,000–340,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, 30,000 Croatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>; Jasenovac was the largest of its 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>.</li><li>Partisan resistance began in the NDH in June 1941, and the state ended in May 1945, its territory becoming part of Democratic Federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a>, named King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-aimone-duke-of-aosta">Tomislav II</a> of the state, never visited it; on learning of his nomination he thought it a bad joke by his cousin King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

