    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> grew out of Croatian resentment of the centralised Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, proclaimed in 1918. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a> was shot in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> parliament in 1928, King Alexander proclaimed a royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in January 1929, and the Ustaša was created in principle that year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-pavelic">Ante Pavelić</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> deputy, went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> gave the Ustaše training grounds in exchange for Pavelić's agreement that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> would go to Italy.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> invaded and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1941. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek">Vladko Maček</a>, leader of the Croatian Peasant Party, refused their offer to form a government. On 10 April 1941, with the German army in control of Zagreb, the retired lieutenant-colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavko-kvaternik">Slavko Kvaternik</a> proclaimed the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in the name of Pavelić. Pavelić returned on 15 April and took power the next day as Poglavnik and prime minister. The Ustaše had fewer than 12,000 members when the war started. Under Italian pressure Pavelić accepted Aimone, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a>, as a figurehead king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-aimone-duke-of-aosta">Tomislav II</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Independent State of Croatia&#8221; cites where it covers Influences on the rise of the Ustaše, Establishment of the NDH, Establishment of borders:</p><ul><li>Jozo Tomasevich, War and Revolution in Yugoslavia 1941-1945: Occupation and Collaboration (Stanford University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The economy was subordinated to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>. A May 1941 agreement gave Germany unrestricted access to industrial raw materials and made the NDH pay for German troops on its soil; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s short-term agreements required supplies, monthly payments and free logging. Both demanded bauxite, iron ore, wood and grain. The Germans took the Ljubija iron mines, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>–Tuzla–Zenica industrial complex and the bauxite, and about 200,000 citizens of the NDH were sent to Germany as slave and forced labourers. In 1942, 80% of exports went to Germany and 12% to Italy.</p><p>Businesses owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> were placed under state commissars, and on 10 December 1941 the postal bank accounts of Serbs, Jews and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> were confiscated. The currency, the kuna, was printed to pay for the upkeep of foreign troops, and circulation rose thirty-two-fold between April 1941 and May 1945. The German Foreign Ministry put the population at about 6,285,000, of whom 52.5% were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and 30.6% Serbs. The Croatian University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> was the only university, and four state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> played in Zagreb, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osijek">Osijek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> and Sarajevo.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Independent State of Croatia&#8221; cites where it covers Economy, Currency, German role, Italian role:</p><ul><li>Rory Yeomans, Visions of Annihilation: The Ustasha Regime and the Cultural Politics of Fascism, 1941–1945 (University of Pittsburgh Press, 2012)</li><li>Jozo Tomasevich, War and Revolution in Yugoslavia 1941-1945: Occupation and Collaboration (Stanford University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Army of the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, the Home Guard, was founded in April 1941 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>'s consent, beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> Militia and a gendarmerie. First limited to 16,000 men, it grew to about 55,000 by mid-1941 and to 130,000 in early 1943. Under the Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> its navy was kept to a few coastal and patrol craft. The air force began with captured Yugoslav aircraft, and its Croatian Air Force Legion fought beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> on the Eastern Front from 1941 to 1943.</p><p>The Partisans led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a>, joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> and others, soon controlled much of the NDH, leaving garrison towns in a de facto state of siege; in 1944 Croats formed 61% of the Partisan units from the Federal State of Croatia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscripts</a>' morale fell, many defected or passed supplies to the Partisans, and the army shrank to 70,000 by late 1944. From 1942 the Ustaše made agreements with Bosnian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a> to fight the Partisans together. In May 1945 about 120,000 NDH troops were handed to the Partisans by the British, and about half were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleiburg-repatriations">Bleiburg repatriations</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Independent State of Croatia&#8221; cites where it covers End of the war, Military:</p><ul><li>Jozo Tomasevich, War and Revolution in Yugoslavia 1941-1945: Occupation and Collaboration (Stanford University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The NDH was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>: every party but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> was banned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-pavelic">Ante Pavelić</a> ruled absolutely as Poglavnik. It was formally a monarchy from the Laws of the Crown of Zvonimir of 15 May 1941, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-aimone-duke-of-aosta">Tomislav II</a> never visited it and abdicated on 31 July 1943. Pavelić then became head of state and in September 1943 appointed Nikola Mandić prime minister. A parliament created by decree on 24 January 1942 was not elected and could not legislate; it met just over a dozen times. The Ustaše Surveillance Service, set up in August 1941, suppressed opposition, and Maček was interned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasenovac-concentration-camp">Jasenovac concentration camp</a>.</p><p>The state was divided into 22 great parishes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-zupan">Grand Župans</a>, with districts, cities and municipalities, and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>'s courts under their older names. From 1941 to 1943 it was split into German and Italian zones. Its laws from April 1941 made any offence to the Croatian nation treason punishable by death and introduced racial laws modelled on Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The minister Milovan Žanić declared in May 1941 that the country must be cleansed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The NDH covered most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and a small part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, the islands, Međimurje and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranja">Baranja</a>. Its main effect on the people within it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. The regime killed an estimated 320,000–340,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, 30,000 Croatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, including between 77,000 and 99,000 people killed at Jasenovac, and forced about 300,000 Serbs out. Jastrebarsko and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Sisak</a> held only children. Jonathan Steinberg called the crimes against Serbs the earliest total genocide attempted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. From May 1943 Croatian Jews were deported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> car to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> also killed and imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> who opposed them; Ivo Goldstein estimates 135,000 Croats died in the NDH, among them Partisans, the regime's opponents and suspected collaborators killed by the Partisans. A German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> report of February 1942 blamed the growth of resistance chiefly on Ustaše atrocities against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> population. The resistance in the NDH and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> made up most of the Partisan movement's strength.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Independent State of Croatia&#8221; cites where it covers Influences on the rise of the Ustaše, Italian influence, Influence of Nazi Germany, Relations with the Chetniks:</p><ul><li>Jozo Tomasevich, War and Revolution in Yugoslavia 1941–1945: The Chetniks, Volume 1 (Stanford University Press, 1975)</li><li>Jozo Tomasevich, War and Revolution in Yugoslavia 1941-1945: Occupation and Collaboration (Stanford University Press, 2001)</li><li>Philip J. Cohen, Serbia's Secret War: Propaganda and the Deceit of History (Texas A&amp;M University Press, 1996)</li><li>Ivo Goldstein, Croatia: A History (McGill-Queen's University Press, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hitler grew angry that Pavelić's policies inflamed rebellion and tied down German troops, and in 1942 he summoned Pavelić to Vinnytsia; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavko-kvaternik">Slavko Kvaternik</a> was replaced and sent into exile. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> capitulated in September 1943, the Italian zone was abolished and the German zone covered the whole state, which annexed the Dalmatian lands ceded in 1941, though much of them was held by the Partisans. In August 1944 Mladen Lorković and Ante Vokić tried a coup to change sides to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>; it failed and they were executed.</p><p>By early 1945 the army withdrew towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> with German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> troops, and the advance of the Partisans and the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> drove a mass retreat towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Pavelić fled, and on 6 May 1945 the regime emptied the bank vaults of the state's gold. The British refused the retreating columns entry into Austria and turned them over to the Partisans; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleiburg-repatriations">Bleiburg repatriations</a> ended in mass executions. On 25 May 1945 the Battle of Odžak ended the state, and its territory became part of Democratic Federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Pavelić died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1959; other ministers were tried with Mile Budak.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> puppet state set up in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in April 1941 and ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-pavelic">Ante Pavelić</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> as a one-party fascist state. It carried out a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, served German and Italian economic demands, and collapsed before the Partisans in May 1945.</em></p>

